.Dd October 28, 2026
.Dt ARNO-PEER 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm arno-peer
.Nd how arno and a text editor talk
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Xr arno 1
and a text editor, an
.Em editor
here, can talk over Unix sockets, each way.
.Bl -tag -width "the editor asks"
.It "the editor asks"
.Nm arno
about lines of a buffer: a question, which becomes a turn, or only the
lines, which go into
.Nm arno Ns 's
input.
.Nm arno
answers the question when the turn ends.
.It "arno calls"
the editor, to see what the human has open, to read a buffer with its
unsaved changes, to open a file at a line, and to make an edit in front of
the human, as one of their own undo steps.
.El
.Pp
The editor in the examples is
.Xr dwa 1 ,
and any program that speaks this page is one.
Neither side needs the other: with no editor,
.Nm arno
reads and writes the disk, and with no
.Nm arno ,
the editor has no one to ask.
.Pp
This page is the protocol: the framing, the sockets and how each side finds
the other, then each direction, then who may do what.
The viewers of a session, and the programs that drive one, speak another
protocol, in
.Xr arno-serve 5 ,
on a socket of their own.
.Sh FRAMING
A connection carries one request and then its answer.
The request is netstrings back to back, the first the verb and the rest its
arguments, and it ends where its sender shuts down its side of writing; the
answer is netstrings too, and ends where the other side closes.
A netstring is a length in decimal, a colon, that many bytes and a comma:
.Ql 5:state, .
.Pp
The first netstring of an answer is its status:
.Bl -tag -width "error" -compact
.It Cm ok
it was done.
.It Cm no
it was understood and the answer is no, as a
.Cm read
of a file that is not open.
.It Cm error
it was not done; the second netstring says why.
.It Cm busy
the human is in the middle of a command, and the request would change what
is on the screen.
Ask again a little later.
.El
.Pp
A reader takes the comma after the last netstring as optional, and a
netstring that is malformed, or longer than what is left, as the end of the
fields.
A field not sent is empty.
Bytes are not interpreted: a field holds whatever it holds, line feeds and
all.
.Pp
A connection that sends nothing is not an error and gets no answer.
That is how
.Nm arno
asks whether an editor is there, and an editor may ask the same of
.Nm arno .
.Pp
A reader waits two seconds at most for the other side, and refuses a request
of more than 16 MiB.
.Nm arno
holds eight connections at once; a ninth pushes out the oldest, so that a
client that connects and says nothing blocks no one.
.Sh SOCKETS AND DISCOVERY
.Nm arno
listens on
.Pa arno- Ns Ar pid Ns Pa .sock
in
.Ev XDG_RUNTIME_DIR ,
made with the umask 077, so that only its user can connect, and removed when
it ends.
With no
.Ev XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
there is no socket, and never one in
.Pa /tmp .
This socket is not the session's viewer socket, which is
.Pa arno/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa .view
in the same directory.
It is made in a terminal's session and under
.Ql "arno \-\-serve" ;
not in a one-shot
.Ql "arno PROMPT" .
.Pp
An editor listens on
.Pa dwa- Ns Ar pid Ns Pa .sock
in the same directory, likewise private and removed at its end.
.Pp
The editor finds
.Nm arno
at
.Ev ARNO_SOCK
if it is set, else at the best
.Pa arno- Ns Ar pid Ns Pa .sock
in the directory of its own socket: the one whose process works in the
editor's directory, before the newest.
.Pp
.Nm arno
calls the editor that asked last; else the socket in
.Ev ARNO_EDITOR ;
else the best
.Pa dwa- Ns Ar pid Ns Pa .sock
in
.Ev XDG_RUNTIME_DIR ,
chosen as above, with the roles turned: the editor whose process works in
.Nm arno Ns 's
directory, before the newest.
A socket of a process that is gone, or of another user's, is passed over,
and a socket at which no one listens is removed.
.Pp
The editor's tools are offered to the model only while an editor is
reachable, which
.Nm arno
asks, by connecting and saying nothing, at the start of each turn, and not
while the session works in the stage's copy, which no editor has open.
.Sh THE EDITOR ASKS ARNO
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm ask Ar question Ar path Ar first Ar last Ar text Op Ar socket Op Cm new
The lines
.Ar first
to
.Ar last
(from 1) of the file
.Ar path
(absolute; empty for a buffer with no name),
.Ar text ,
each ended by a line feed, as the editor has them, unsaved changes and all,
and what the human typed about them,
.Ar question .
.Ar socket
is the editor's own socket, where the answer goes; empty, there is none.
.Cm new ,
in the last field, asks for a new conversation, started first.
.El
.Pp
What
.Nm arno
does with it:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It A question
becomes a turn, run now or, if
.Nm arno
is in a turn, after it.
The answer is
.Cm ok
with
.Ql asked
or, in a turn,
.Ql "queued; arno is busy" ;
the answer to the question itself comes later, as
.Cm reply .
.It No question
puts a reference to the lines,
.Ql @path:first-last
from
.Nm arno Ns 's directory, into
.Nm arno Ns 's input, after what is typed there, for the human to finish.
The answer is
.Cm ok
with
.Ql "added to the input" .
.It Cm new
with no question starts the new conversation and puts the reference in the
input; with one, the turn is in the new conversation.
.El
.Pp
The editor that asked is the one
.Nm arno
calls from then on, if it named a socket.
.Pp
When the turn ends,
.Nm arno
calls the editor at
.Ar socket
with
.Cm reply ,
the question and the model's answer, as the editor's own
.Cm reply
below takes them.
A turn that failed, or was stopped, sends none.
.Pp
An ask is refused, as
.Cm error
with the reason, and run as no turn and put in no input, if:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
it has fewer than the six fields up to
.Ar text ;
.It
its connection is another user's;
.It
this
.Nm arno
belongs to a host, as under
.Ql "arno \-\-serve" :
the answer is
.Ql "this arno belongs to NAME; ask your own" ,
since a question typed in the editor belongs in the human's own
.Nm arno ,
not in one a program is driving;
.It
its
.Ar socket
is not a socket directly in
.Nm arno Ns 's socket directory (see
.Sx SECURITY ) ;
.It
its
.Ar path
holds a control character.
.El
.Pp
An ask never passes as something the human typed at the terminal.
In the transcript it is a line from the editor, with the path and the lines,
and the question under it; the model is given it under a first line that
says it came from the editor and not from the terminal, and its system
prompt says what that means.
.Sh ARNO CALLS THE EDITOR
Each request is one connection, and
.Nm arno
calls again a quarter of a second after a
.Cm busy ,
for about fifteen seconds, or until the human stops the turn.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm state
Answers
.Cm ok
and a text: the editor's directory, the current buffer and its line, what the
human is doing if it is not normal mode, and every buffer open, with the
unsaved ones marked.
It is the text of the tool
.Ic editor .
.It Cm buffers
Answers
.Cm ok
and then one more netstring for each buffer that holds a file, its absolute
path.
.Nm arno
does not ask it.
.It Cm read Ar path
The whole text of the buffer of the file at
.Ar path ,
absolute or from the editor's directory, or of the current buffer if
.Ar path
is empty, unsaved changes and all, each line ended by a line feed.
.Cm no
if the file is not open.
.Nm arno
uses it for the tool
.Ic read ,
for the text an
.Ic edit
is made on, and to refuse to move or remove a file that is open.
.It Cm open Ar path Ar line
Shows the file at
.Ar path ,
the cursor on
.Ar line
(from 1; none or 0, where it was).
Answers
.Cm ok
and what it did, or
.Cm error
if there is no such file.
It is the tool
.Ic open .
.It Cm edit Ar path Ar old Ar new Op Cm all
Replaces
.Ar old
by
.Ar new
in the file at
.Ar path :
once, and
.Cm error
if
.Ar old
is not there or is there more than once, or, with
.Cm all
as the last field, everywhere.
An empty
.Ar old
makes the file, of
.Ar new .
The file is opened if it was not, and saved if it had no unsaved changes
before; the change is one undo step.
Answers
.Cm ok
and what it did, or
.Cm error
and why.
The human approves the edit in
.Nm arno
before it is asked of the editor, as any edit; a refusal is never sent.
.It Cm reply Ar question Ar text
.Nm arno Ns 's answer to a question the editor asked, with the question again,
so that the editor can show what the answer is to.
The editor keeps it until it can show it, and answers
.Cm ok
at once, even when it is
.Cm busy
for the verbs above.
.El
.Pp
A verb an editor does not know is answered
.Cm error ,
and
.Nm arno
does with the disk what the editor lacks.
.Sh SECURITY
The sockets are in the user's own runtime directory, made with the umask 077,
and a connection from another user is refused: on
.Nm arno Ns 's
socket by asking the system who is on the other end
.Pq Dv SO_PEERCRED ,
and it is answered
.Cm error
and closed.
Beyond that, any program of the same user that can reach the socket can send
an ask, and the protocol does not pretend otherwise.
So:
.Bl -bullet
.It
An ask is never the human's message.
It is shown as the editor's, is put to the model as the editor's, with the
lines said to be quoted text, never instructions, and decides nothing:
every edit, move, removal and program run that follows is approved at the
terminal, as for any turn.
.It
The answer to a question can go only where the editor's own socket is.
.Nm arno
gives it to the socket the ask names, and that must be a socket directly in
.Nm arno Ns 's
socket directory, not in a directory below it and not outside it, whose name
is
.Ar name Ns Pa - Ns Ar pid Ns Pa .sock ,
with
.Ar pid
the number of a process that is running and is the user's, which is the
user's own file and not a symbolic link, nor a regular file or a directory.
Otherwise the ask is refused, so that an ask cannot make
.Nm arno
send the answer, which may hold the model's reading of the user's files, to
any other file or program.
.It
The path in an ask holds no control character, so that it cannot make a
second line of the transcript or of the message.
.It
.Nm arno
calls only sockets the user owns.
What an editor answers to
.Cm read
goes to the model, as what the human has open does.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
The ask of the lines 2 and 3 of
.Pa /home/me/a.c ,
with a question, from an editor whose socket is
.Pa /run/user/1000/dwa-4242.sock ,
is these netstrings, then the editor's shutdown:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
3:ask,4:why?,12:/home/me/a.c,1:2,1:3,8:one\entwo\en,
31:/run/user/1000/dwa-4242.sock,0:,
.Ed
.Pp
and the answer
.Bd -literal -offset indent
2:ok,5:asked,
.Ed
.Pp
Later arno calls the editor with
.Bd -literal -offset indent
5:reply,4:why?,7:Because.,
.Ed
.Pp
From a shell with
.Xr nc 1 ,
which can shut down its side of writing, a check that
.Nm arno
is there, and an ask with no question:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
printf '3:ask,0:,0:,1:1,1:1,0:,' | nc \-UN $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/arno-4243.sock
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr arno 1 ,
.Xr arno-serve 5 ,
.Xr dwa 1
.Pp
D. J. Bernstein,
.Lk https://cr.yp.to/proto/netstrings.txt "Netstrings" ,
1997.
.Sh HISTORY
The framing, the verbs and the discovery are those of the Pascal arno's
editor protocol, so that an editor written for it works with this
.Nm arno .
What is new is the security: the ask that cannot pass as the human's
message, the reply socket checked, and the socket that is the user's
alone.
.Sh CAVEATS
The reply socket is checked when the ask comes, and used when the turn ends.
.Pp
Whether the file named is a socket is not told by the system call
.Nm arno
uses: it refuses a symbolic link, a regular file and a directory, and a
named pipe or a device the user owns, named like a socket, would be taken
for one; connecting to it, which is all that is done, does nothing.
.Pp
The process number in the name is not checked to be the asking process's:
an editor that asks from a helper process is allowed.
.Pp
The size of a Unix socket's path is limited by the system to 107 bytes; a
runtime directory with a longer name has no socket.
